WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:12.750
[Dr. Terry:] There are more than 140,000 patient
admissions to the hospitals

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.510
of the Public Health Service every year.

00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:20.300
Our patients include American seamen like
ship's carpenter Danny Callahan,

00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:23.680
who received care at the Boston Hospital,

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:30.260
officers and enlisted men of the US Coast
Guard, Coast and Geodetic Survey, other uniformed services

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:36.260
and their dependents, American Indians, Alaskan natives, federal prisoners,

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:40.860
and other groups designated by the Congress.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.610
We also provide medical education for interns,
residents, medical students,

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:48.260
and many others in our hospitals.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:52.890
The first responsibility of our hospitals
is the health of our patients.

00:00:52.890 --> 00:00:57.000
The second responsibility is medical education.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:02.100
Medical research is one of the most important
factors in providing the best patient care,

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:04.290
and the best medical education.

00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:09.350
Today, several of our hospitals are conducting
research in association with medical schools

00:01:09.350 --> 00:01:11.210
and other institutions.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:17.100
For example, the Public Health Service hospital
in New Orleans has developed a comprehensive

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:23.440
research and training program in cooperation
with the medical school at Tulane University.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:29.360
One of the people who recently played a major
role in this program is Dr. John L. Wilson,

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.840
former medical officer-in-charge at New Orleans
and who is now retired from the service.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.880
Through the enterprise of Dr. Wilson, this
building on the hospital grounds

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:44.890
was converted into a research facility, the
Seamen's Memorial Laboratory.

00:01:44.890 --> 00:01:48.590
The director of research at New Orleans is
Dr. John J. Walsh,

00:01:48.590 --> 00:01:51.150
who received his research training at Tulane.

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:56.180
I've asked Dr. Walsh and his colleagues to
give us a brief report on their program.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:03.380
[Dr. Walsh:] We could define the research
activities in the Public Health Service hospitals

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:10.259
as directed towards one objective, that of
better patient care,

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:16.420
with the inevitable accompaniment of better professional training and better professional status.

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:23.590
It has been our philosophy to have a diversified
program not dependent upon one man,

00:02:23.590 --> 00:02:26.350
nor upon one discipline.

00:02:26.350 --> 00:02:35.870
Currently, we have approximately 4000 square
feet of laboratory space and 31 fulltime people

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:38.060
working in our research laboratory.

00:02:38.060 --> 00:02:45.060
It has been our feeling that these fulltime
academicians and fulltime investigators

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.880
should be available to the hospital for teaching and consultative purposes,

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:59.280
and also to help to create the atmosphere of intellectual stimulation and curiosity

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:02.080
so necessary if we would make a hospital

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:08.510
a teaching institution and not a trade school
in which merely techniques are learned.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:15.520
Briefly, our overall program is as follows.

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:23.320
There is a tropical medical section, which
is an integral component

00:03:23.330 --> 00:03:28.030
of the Department of Tropical Medicine and Public Health at Tulane.

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:33.720
Work in this area is primarily concerned with
natural and acquired resistance

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.020
to parasitic infections.

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:45.959
In the second of our three major sections,
research endeavors

00:03:45.959 --> 00:03:50.099
are oriented towards the surgical field.

00:03:50.099 --> 00:03:57.800
This project is concerned currently with the
application of regional perfusion

00:03:57.800 --> 00:03:59.300
to the treatment of malignancies.

00:04:01.360 --> 00:04:09.100
The third major area of investigation which
concerns our laboratory is that concerned

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:17.020
with cardiovascular diseases and specifically with studies of the dilated heart.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:22.560
[Dr. Terry:] The research on the dilated heart
at New Orleans is under the personal direction

00:04:22.569 --> 00:04:27.819
of Dr. Walsh and Dr. George E. Burch, professor
and chairmen of the Department of Medicine

00:04:27.819 --> 00:04:28.819
at Tulane.

00:04:28.820 --> 00:04:32.979
Dr. Burch is known around the world for his
research in heart disease.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:39.680
[Dr. George E. Burch:] As we know, one of
the great problems in cardiology is the problem

00:04:39.680 --> 00:04:41.439
of a large heart,

00:04:41.439 --> 00:04:45.930
Most patients who die of heart disease either
die because of congestive heart failure,

00:04:45.930 --> 00:04:52.509
which is as a rule associated with a large
dilated heart, or from coronary disease

00:04:52.509 --> 00:04:55.940
with myocardial ischemia.

00:04:55.940 --> 00:05:05.020
Our main interest is to try to first define
as well as we can what a large heart is.

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:10.379
We're attempting to approach this both from
the anatomical point of view,

00:05:10.379 --> 00:05:16.099
not necessarily just gross anatomy, but from the point of view of detailed microscopic studies.

00:05:17.189 --> 00:05:20.279
as well as from the point of view of electron microscopy.

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:29.300
In addition, a chemical definition will be attempted of trying to define the inorganic chemical state

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:37.880
of the dilated heart as well as certain aspects of organic chemical changes.

00:05:37.880 --> 00:05:43.100
In addition to that, an effort is being made
to treat the dilated heart,

00:05:43.100 --> 00:05:46.160
particularly in patients who have so-called intractable failure,

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:50.960
where efforts have been made to use usual conventional

00:05:50.969 --> 00:05:55.520
therapeutic procedures and still the patient
seems to go downhill.

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:00.100
One of the ideas that has been proving to
be of considerable success

00:06:00.120 --> 00:06:02.720
is the idea of long bed rest.

00:06:02.729 --> 00:06:07.520
Patients have been placed in bed for periods varying from six months to a year or even more,

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:13.210
and so far this has been proving to be of
considerable value to these patients

00:06:13.210 --> 00:06:16.529
who would have been dead if it were not for
this period of rest.

00:06:16.529 --> 00:06:21.849
Unfortunately, this does take a great deal
of time in order to see through enough patients

00:06:21.849 --> 00:06:27.159
to have an idea as to what the statistical
significance of all this is, but even with

00:06:27.159 --> 00:06:28.919
a few number of patients,

00:06:28.920 --> 00:06:34.900
we know so far that this has a great deal
to offer at the moment.

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:39.840
[Dr. Terry:] Research at the New Orleans hospital
includes several disciplines.

00:06:39.849 --> 00:06:42.789
One of these is biochemistry.

00:06:42.789 --> 00:06:47.719
Here is Dr. Joseph Arcos, associate professor
of biochemistry at Tulane,

00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:50.400
to explain the work being done in this field.

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:54.619
[Dr. Joseph Arcos:] The program of the biochemistry
section centers on the study

00:06:54.619 --> 00:06:58.639
of alterations in endoplasmic cellular membranes.

00:06:58.639 --> 00:07:03.539
The same techniques are applied to investigate
these alterations during the process

00:07:03.539 --> 00:07:08.909
of heart dilatation and during chemical carcinogenesis in the liver.

00:07:08.909 --> 00:07:13.460
At the present time, we are studying the mitochondrial
membrane by following

00:07:13.460 --> 00:07:18.580
changes in the swelling ability and shape
of this cell particle.

00:07:18.580 --> 00:07:22.909
The first slide shows a phase of the procedure
for isolation of mitochondria.

00:07:22.909 --> 00:07:28.719
All these operations involving living tissues
are carried out at near zero degrees centigrade

00:07:28.719 --> 00:07:30.520
in the cold room.

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:36.080
Incidentally, the young man pictured here
is a second year medical student at Tulane University

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:40.949
School of Medicine who is receiving research
orientation while conducting

00:07:40.949 --> 00:07:46.909
a [?] research project at the U.S. Public
Health Hospital Research Laboratory.

00:07:46.909 --> 00:07:52.439
The second slide shows members of our group
injecting into the guinea pig deoxycorticosterone

00:07:52.440 --> 00:07:58.749
which produces heart dilatation following
[?] administration.

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:03.760
The third slide shows the measuring by photometry
of the pattern of mitochondria swelling.

00:08:04.680 --> 00:08:08.979
The investigator pictured here is a science
student from Formosa

00:08:08.979 --> 00:08:15.849
taking part in the research training program
of Tulane University Medical School.

00:08:15.849 --> 00:08:21.139
The fourth slide illustrates an extension
of this approach; that is, to study changes

00:08:21.139 --> 00:08:27.050
in the shape of the mitochondria such as elongation,
shortening, or curling.

00:08:27.050 --> 00:08:30.959
Such alterations are determined by following
the particulate dissymmetry

00:08:30.959 --> 00:08:33.800
by light scattering at various wavelengths.

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:40.610
Finally, the last slide shows a phase of another
research orientation of our program.

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:45.940
Here, a change is produced by various chemical agents in the macromolecular fine structure

00:08:45.940 --> 00:08:51.630
of proteins are studied by following in a
polarimeter, the optical rotation of the solutions.

00:08:51.630 --> 00:08:56.900
In this way, we hope to obtain information
on the mechanism of drug action

00:08:56.900 --> 00:08:59.230
at the basic macromolecular level.

00:08:59.230 --> 00:09:04.440
[Dr. Terry:] Although prolonged bed rest is
a basic therapy in the treatment of the patients

00:09:04.440 --> 00:09:11.220
participating in the dilated heart studies
at New Orleans, drug therapy is also important.

00:09:11.220 --> 00:09:15.700
The pharmacology studies are under the direction of Dr. Thomas D. Darby,

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:18.260
Associate Professor of Pharmacology at Tulane.

00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:24.371
[Dr. Thomas D. Darby:] Investigations with
intact hearts and with hearts in situ are

00:09:24.380 --> 00:09:26.980
are now in progress in our laboratories.

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:33.820
In this slide, an isolated heart is demonstrated.

00:09:33.820 --> 00:09:42.480
An Anderson isolated heart perfusion apparatus is used in combination with a bubble-type oxygenator

00:09:42.880 --> 00:09:46.280
and Sigma motor pump system.

00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:51.840
In these studies, the coronary arteries are
perfused with oxygenated blood

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:55.580
obtained from donor animals.

00:09:55.580 --> 00:10:01.720
The purpose of these experiments is to determine
the direct effects of a drug or a combination

00:10:01.720 --> 00:10:06.520
of drugs on the force of contraction of heart
muscle.

00:10:06.520 --> 00:10:14.360
In the next slide, an experiment is being
carried out with a heart in situ.

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:19.870
In this study, direct measurements of the
drugs on the force of contraction of the heart

00:10:19.870 --> 00:10:25.270
are being compared with hemodynamic parameters.

00:10:25.270 --> 00:10:29.940
In this study, a total body perfusion is carried out.

00:10:29.940 --> 00:10:37.280
Blood is removed from the right atrium of
the dog and returned to the left atrium of the dog.

00:10:37.860 --> 00:10:42.210
In this way the right ventricle and lungs
are bypassed.

00:10:42.210 --> 00:10:48.080
The blood is oxygenated in a bubble-type oxygenator
and pumped back to a reservoir

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:51.960
which returns the blood to the left atrium.

00:10:52.760 --> 00:11:01.240
Measurements of isometric contractility, isotonic
contractility, and all-force velocity curves

00:11:01.250 --> 00:11:05.300
can be obtained from the non-working ventricle.

00:11:05.300 --> 00:11:12.510
These measurements are compared with isometric
tension in the working ventricle.

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:17.760
In addition to the biochemical, physiological,
and pharmacological studies,

00:11:17.760 --> 00:11:21.930
clinical studies of drug effects are also
in progress.

00:11:21.930 --> 00:11:26.660
One of the medical residents is largely engaged
in the clinical studies

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:30.260
and takes part in the laboratory experiments.

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:35.190
The residents are an important part of our
applied research program.

00:11:35.190 --> 00:11:42.140
They are daily in contact with therapeutic
problems and are willing to work to obtain the answers.

00:11:43.180 --> 00:11:47.260
The training in our laboratories is also multidiscipline.

00:11:47.270 --> 00:11:52.700
In addition to the Public Health Service personnel, there are two graduate students in pharmacology

00:11:52.700 --> 00:11:59.060
from Tulane University, and two medical students
from LSU receiving training here.

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:05.420
[Dr. Terry:] A total of 30 patients have taken
part in the dilated heart studies at New Orleans

00:12:05.430 --> 00:12:09.430
and 20 of these have now been discharged.

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:14.220
Here is the x-ray taken of the heart of Mrs.
Erma Francis when she was admitted to the hospital

00:12:14.760 --> 00:12:18.900
as a special study patient for this research
program.

00:12:18.900 --> 00:12:25.030
Here is her heart as it is today, and here
are the two x-rays side by side.

00:12:25.030 --> 00:12:30.140
Dr. Walsh visited Mrs. Francis and interviewed
her where she is now employed.

00:12:31.000 --> 00:12:35.240
[Dr. John J. Walsh:] Erma I think our listeners
might like to know where you're working.

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:36.470
Would you mind telling us please?

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:41.550
[Erma Francis:] I work at Chalmette Laundry, one of the largest laundries in the city of New Orleans.

00:12:42.550 --> 00:12:48.160
[Dr. John J. Walsh:] Erma, what kind of symptoms
were you having before you went into the

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:49.960
New Orleans Public Health Service Hospital?

00:12:49.960 --> 00:12:57.860
[Erma Francis:] I was having severe pain in
the chest, between the bust you know,

00:12:57.860 --> 00:13:05.960
and the stomach down here, and I was very
swollen and full of fluids and my feet and legs

00:13:06.140 --> 00:13:09.560
and everything were swollen and I had vomiting
spells.

00:13:09.560 --> 00:13:13.101
[Dr. John J. Walsh:] And you had had heart
trouble for some time before that,

00:13:13.101 --> 00:13:14.101
hadn't you, Erma?

00:13:14.101 --> 00:13:15.101
[Erma Francis:] Yes I had.

00:13:15.101 --> 00:13:24.440
I found that I had an enlarged heart since
1937, and from 1958 I had trouble, trouble on and off,

00:13:24.660 --> 00:13:32.420
in and out of the hospital until November,
around November 1958, I was sent to Charity,

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:40.280
and then from Charity I went to Public Health Hospital, and for about six months to nine months

00:13:40.300 --> 00:13:42.840
I didn't do anything but rest.

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:49.020
Then after that I began to go down, go to
the bathroom and walk around,

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:53.640
until I was discharged in August 1959.

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:58.500
[Dr. John J. Walsh:] Now since you've gotten
out of the hospital Erma,

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:00.150
have you had any trouble with your heart?

00:14:00.150 --> 00:14:04.640
[Erma Francis:] I haven't had any trouble,
I've been feeling fine and I work every day,

00:14:04.640 --> 00:14:07.280
you see the kind of work that I am doing,
okay?

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:09.620
[Dr. John J. Walsh:] And how hot is it in
here right now?

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:12.120
[Erma Francis:] It's really hot right now,
but it doesn't bother me.

00:14:12.120 --> 00:14:15.000
[Dr. John J. Walsh:] I understand it's 109 right now.

00:14:15.000 --> 00:14:16.070
[Erma Francis:] Yeah.

00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:22.090
[Dr. Terry:] Here's another photo of Ms. Francis
taken recently at the New Orleans hospital.

00:14:22.090 --> 00:14:26.440
She still takes a great deal of interest in
the heart project and returns nearly every week

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:29.320
to help with the care of patients.

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:35.010
One of the resident physicians now working on the cancer project is Dr. James N. Winblad,

00:14:35.010 --> 00:14:36.930
a resident in surgery.

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:40.260
Dr. Winblad began his research in cancer perfusion.

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:44.160
[Dr. James N. Winblad:] If you had asked me
if I would want to go down to Tulane

00:14:44.160 --> 00:14:51.840
and spend a year in relative isolation working on a basic research program that perhaps had no direct

00:14:51.880 --> 00:14:58.000
clinical bearing or no direct clinical relationship, I would have been relatively uninterested.

00:14:58.000 --> 00:15:03.750
But what happened was that we were able to
work on a relatively basic problem, that is

00:15:03.750 --> 00:15:10.600
the development of techniques for isolation perfusion of extremities and other regions of the body

00:15:11.820 --> 00:15:17.120
and then apply this to the treatment of human cancer in a relatively short time,

00:15:17.120 --> 00:15:20.350
that is, in a span of relatively few months.

00:15:20.350 --> 00:15:26.520
We developed the technique in dogs for extracorporeal
circulation and regional perfusion,

00:15:26.520 --> 00:15:30.279
and then applied it in the treatment of human
patients with cancer.

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:35.920
[Dr. Terry:] The present medical officer-in-charge at New Orleans is Dr. John A. Trautman,

00:15:35.920 --> 00:15:38.700
who has served at several Public Health Service hospitals

00:15:38.700 --> 00:15:43.080
and was formerly director of the Clinical Center in Bethesda, Maryland.

00:15:43.080 --> 00:15:49.880
[Dr. John A. Trautman:] I considered that
research over the years in our hospitals as one of the

00:15:49.920 --> 00:15:55.940
best recruiting devices that we could possibly have, and I believe that if the service

00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:57.180
is to go forward

00:15:57.180 --> 00:16:07.120
and to attract proper personnel, career people to carry on the work of the service, that the service

00:16:07.760 --> 00:16:16.120
will have to develop its research facilities
in our hospitals to the utmost.

00:16:16.130 --> 00:16:22.930
We are leaders in the field of medicine not
only in the United States, but in the world.

00:16:22.930 --> 00:16:29.560
It behooves is us in the service to have the
best possible research facilities,

00:16:29.760 --> 00:16:38.780
the best hospitals, to accomplish the aims which every good clinical facility should have.

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:43.940
[Dr. Terry:] When we began organizing this
present research program at our New Orleans hospital,

00:16:45.100 --> 00:16:49.470
we were fortunate to have the fullest possible cooperation and encouragement

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:54.600
from one of the nation's leaders in medical
education, Dr. Maxwell Lapham,

00:16:54.800 --> 00:16:56.420
dean of Tulane Medical School.

00:16:56.430 --> 00:17:00.240
[Dr. Maxwell Lapham:] The close affiliation
of the U.S. Public Service Hospital

00:17:00.240 --> 00:17:04.799
with the school of medicine at Tulane University
has many important facets.

00:17:04.800 --> 00:17:11.320
In the first place it unifies two institutions which are dedicated to medical training and medical care.

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:16.040
It allows for the interchange of staff and
facilities,

00:17:16.140 --> 00:17:19.480
specifically the facilities of the Public
Health Service hospital

00:17:19.480 --> 00:17:24.660
are of tremendous help to the school
of medicine here, because our students and faculty

00:17:24.760 --> 00:17:31.140
can work with the different kinds of patients from far-flung areas and also learn more about

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:35.160
the objectives and responsibilities of the
Public Health Service hospitals.

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:41.560
Moreover, the hospital staff members who have medical school appointments have assisted greatly

00:17:41.560 --> 00:17:43.420
in our teaching programs.

00:17:43.800 --> 00:17:49.600
On the side of the hospital staff, there is
opportunity to work with men who are dedicated

00:17:49.720 --> 00:17:56.880
not only to medical training and medical service
but to pursuing basic and clinical research.

00:17:56.880 --> 00:18:01.899
Many of the hospital staff members find a
deep satisfaction in pursuing a more or less

00:18:01.899 --> 00:18:08.150
academic career within the framework of their
Public Health Service responsibilities.

00:18:08.150 --> 00:18:13.409
This should be of assistance in recruiting
people into careers in the health service

00:18:13.409 --> 00:18:15.630
throughout the country.

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:20.080
They will have the satisfaction of knowing
that in addition to rendering superior medical care,

00:18:21.080 --> 00:18:26.360
they are contributing to medical knowledge
through research within both institutions.

00:18:27.580 --> 00:18:32.780
[Dr. Terry:] The Public Health Service is
indebted to Dean Lapham, to Dr. George Burch, and to other

00:18:33.000 --> 00:18:37.740
members of the Tulane Medical School for their
generous cooperation, their enthusiasm and

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:43.620
encouragement in helping us to carry forward
the research program at New Orleans.

00:18:44.300 --> 00:18:49.320
In addition to New Orleans, we're conducting
research in other Public Health Service hospitals,

00:18:49.320 --> 00:18:54.210
in the Indian Health Program, and in the hospitals
in the federal prisons.

00:18:54.210 --> 00:19:00.040
One of the most important types of research we're now conducting is the inter-hospital project

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:04.840
where several Public Health Service hospitals join together on a common problem

00:19:04.840 --> 00:19:06.460
with a single protocol.

00:19:07.320 --> 00:19:10.220
Here is the Public Health Service hospital
at Staten Island,

00:19:10.220 --> 00:19:15.520
which serves as administrative center for
the first inter-hospital project.

00:19:15.940 --> 00:19:22.040
Seven hospitals are now conducting investigations
in the clinical pharmacology of various drugs

00:19:22.049 --> 00:19:24.999
designed for the treatment of essential hypertension.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:31.640
These inter-hospital investigations will help
to speed up the testing of new drugs.

00:19:31.880 --> 00:19:36.340
Our Baltimore hospital pioneered heart research
in the service in cooperation

00:19:36.340 --> 00:19:42.389
with the National Heart Institute and also
has an important program in cancer chemotherapy

00:19:42.389 --> 00:19:46.419
in cooperation with the National Cancer Institute.

00:19:46.419 --> 00:19:50.850
Our Seattle hospital and the medical school
of the University of Washington

00:19:50.850 --> 00:19:54.549
are setting up a cooperative research program.

00:19:54.549 --> 00:19:59.369
Our San Francisco hospital is working out
details for research and association

00:19:59.369 --> 00:20:04.330
with a major medical center and with the medical
school.

00:20:04.330 --> 00:20:09.019
Research in the Indian hospitals has developed around the clinical health problems of the Indians.

00:20:10.020 --> 00:20:16.880
For example, at the present time pilot studies are being planned or conducted on diabetes, anemia,

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:24.320
cervical cancer, nutrition, trachoma, otitis
media, and diseases of the gallbladder.

00:20:25.120 --> 00:20:30.620
Since 1930, the Public Health Service has
had responsibility for directing the medical program

00:20:30.720 --> 00:20:36.120
of the Bureau of Prisons and the Department
of Justice, which maintains 22 hospitals.

00:20:36.120 --> 00:20:41.090
For several years, we have conducted research
projects in many fields of investigation

00:20:41.090 --> 00:20:44.659
with the aid of volunteer prisoner patients.

00:20:44.660 --> 00:20:49.500
It is the aim of the Public Health Service
to develop these research activities in our hospitals

00:20:49.640 --> 00:20:54.760
in order to provide our patients with the
best possible medical care and to enhance

00:20:54.900 --> 00:20:57.480
the quality of the medical education.

00:20:57.480 --> 00:21:02.760
Research also provides the opportunity for
physicians and others in the service to extend

00:21:02.800 --> 00:21:07.920
the range of their skills and to add greater depth to their professional judgment.

00:21:07.920 --> 00:21:13.220
It is essential that we make these opportunities
available because our ultimate success

00:21:13.300 --> 00:21:19.380
in fulfilling our responsibilities for the health of the nation depends directly upon the skill,

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:25.100
the wisdom, and the dedication of the men and women in the Public Health Service.

